WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.651
An AI-generated fix passed one hundred and fifty-five selected tests.

00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:08.432
It still missed a second implementation. This is Patch Audit.

00:00:10.756 --> 00:00:15.236
These are real saved results from Boltons, a Python utility library.

00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:20.937
The ordinary suite passed. Open its execution log to inspect the retained
output.

00:00:22.344 --> 00:00:28.893
The task required both copies of the ordered multi-dictionary to handle one-shot
iterables consistently.

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:32.909
That requirement was specified separately from the candidate patch.

00:00:34.432 --> 00:00:37.696
The exact patch collects input once, then reuses it.

00:00:37.936 --> 00:00:43.782
But the change affects only dictutils. The standalone copy in urlutils was left
untouched.

00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:49.730
Here is that untouched implementation. It consumes the iterable twice.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:54.130
Normal package imports can hide the fallback used in standalone mode.

00:00:55.911 --> 00:01:03.485
The independent empty-input check expected length zero. It observed length one,
even though keys and items were empty.

00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:06.626
These are observations of one incomplete repair.

00:01:08.292 --> 00:01:11.300
Download the original evidence and exact patch.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.599
Source, candidate, runtime and verifier identities bind the outcome to what
actually ran.

00:01:18.943 --> 00:01:23.722
Public visitors can inspect this saved evidence, but cannot execute patches.

00:01:23.962 --> 00:01:31.087
The separate G3 study found zero additional independent issues across six
historical developer patches.

00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:39.618
To audit a new change, follow the local Docker quickstart.

00:01:39.858 --> 00:01:47.581
Review immutable source, the patch and declared checks. Validate controls and
approve the exact plans before execution.
